## Burgundy and Wine: The Fall Color That Never Fades

Burgundy is the one <strong>fall nail color</strong> that shows up on every list, every year, and somehow never feels stale. There is a reason for that. It does the same job a good leather jacket does. It works with everything, it never looks like you tried too hard, and it reads as intentional even when you picked it in thirty seconds at the drugstore.

This year's version leans slightly more purple than red, closer to a true merlot than a classic wine. Cooler skin tones get the most out of it, since the purple undertone plays nicely against pink and neutral undertones in the skin. That said, deep burgundy and deep red are perfect for light skin too, adding contrast and warmth rather than washing anyone out, which is part of why the shade keeps earning its spot on this list rather than getting swapped out for something newer.

At the salon, ask for a "merlot" or "wine" gel, and mention you want it glossy rather than matte if you are after that classic, slightly wet-look finish. Two to three coats gives full, rich opacity. On gel or dip powder, expect this shade to hold for two to three weeks before you notice any real fade. For a fuller exploration of burgundy-specific manicure ideas, from ombre to accent nails, <a href='/blog/fall/burgundy-fall-nails'>burgundy nails for fall</a> goes further than colour alone. If you want it faded rather than solid, burgundy-to-rose is one of the most reliable <a href='/blog/fall/fall-ombre-nails'>fall ombre nails</a> combinations to blend clean on the first attempt.

