## Finding Your Fall Nail Color by Skin Tone

Choosing <strong>fall nail colors</strong> for your skin tone comes down to undertone, not depth. Cool undertones (pink or blue casts to the skin) pair best with burgundy, plum, and true jewel tones, while warm undertones (golden or peachy casts) lean best into mocha, terracotta, and burnt orange. Neutral undertones can borrow from either side.

You already know the frustration this solves. "I don't know if a color will suit my skin tone until it's already on" is close to the most common complaint about fall polish shopping, and it is exactly why skin-tone guidance belongs inside this list rather than off in a separate article you have to go dig up.

On paler skin, deep, saturated shades create the most flattering contrast. Burgundy and deep red genuinely are two of the most reliable choices here, since they add colour and warmth rather than competing with an already-light complexion. A rich chocolate also works better than a flat, skin-matching nude, which can occasionally wash out fair skin rather than complementing it.

On medium and olive skin tones, warm caramel, sandy beige, and burnt orange all read as effortless rather than trying too hard, since the golden undertone in the skin pulls the same warmth out of the polish. Jewel tones, plum and emerald especially, also read as rich rather than muddy against this depth of skin.

On deep skin tones, colour genuinely opens up. Jewel-toned blues and greens look striking against darker complexions, colours with a hint of orange add real warmth and depth, and there is no single shade considered unflattering the way there sometimes is at the paler end of the spectrum. The one thing worth avoiding is a flat, light grey with no blue undertone, which can occasionally read as dull rather than sophisticated. A moodier, blue-based grey solves that instantly.

If you only remember one rule from this section, it is this: match undertone before you match depth. A cool-toned burgundy on warm skin, or a warm terracotta on cool skin, will always look slightly off no matter how popular the shade is this season.

