## How to Ask for the Right Shade at the Salon

The fastest way to get the <strong>fall nail colors</strong> you actually pictured is to bring one reference photo and name the undertone you want, warmer or cooler than your last manicure, rather than describing the colour from memory. Nail techs see hundreds of shades a week, and "kind of a dark red" means something different to every single one of them.

A script that works almost every time: "I want a fall shade that's a little warmer or deeper than what I usually get, something like [name a shade from this list]. What formula do you have that's closest to that?" Bring the photo, not just the name. A screenshot gets you a far closer match than any verbal description ever will, and it also gives your tech an instant read on finish, whether you are picturing matte, glossy, or chrome.

It is worth asking directly about formula, too. Gel or dip powder holds dark, opaque colours like espresso or blackened plum considerably longer than regular polish, which tends to chip and show wear at the tips first, usually within a week. If longevity matters more to you than switching colours every few weeks, say so before your tech even reaches for a bottle.

